var definitions = ['<h2>Tu es parti avant moi.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when talking to one person in an informal context. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>es</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". Here, it is used as an auxiliary verb to form the past tense.</div><div><strong>parti</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "partir", which means "to leave". In this sentence, it’s used in combination with the auxiliary verb "es" to indicate past action, specifically that you (the subject) left.</div><div><strong>avant</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "before". It’s used to indicate temporal order, suggesting that the action of the subject occurred earlier than that of the object.</div><div><strong>moi</strong> This is a strong (stressed) pronoun in French, equivalent to "me" in English. It’s used here to indicate that the subject left before the person speaking.</div>','<h2>Je m’étire avant de courir.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>m’étire</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the reflexive verb "s’étirer", which means "to stretch oneself". The "m’" before the verb indicates that it is reflexive, meaning the action is being done by the subject to themselves.</div><div><strong>avant</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "before" in English. It indicates that the action of stretching occurs prior to another action.</div><div><strong>de</strong> In this context, "de" is a preposition used in conjunction with "avant" to introduce the infinitive of the following verb.</div><div><strong>courir</strong> This is the French infinitive verb meaning "to run". When following "avant de", it describes the action that will take place after the subject has finished stretching.</div>','<h2>Il se rase avant de prendre sa douche.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>se</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun that corresponds with "il". In French, reflexive pronouns are used with reflexive verbs to indicate that the subject of the verb is performing an action on itself.</div><div><strong>rase</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the reflexive verb "se raser", which means "to shave oneself". It indicates an action that the subject is performing on themselves.</div><div><strong>avant de</strong> This phrase means "before" in English. It’s used to indicate that one action is performed prior to another.</div><div><strong>prendre</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "prendre", which means "to take". In this context, it’s part of the phrase "prendre sa douche", which means "to take his shower".</div><div><strong>sa</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "his" in English. It indicates that the shower is associated with the male subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>douche</strong> This is a French noun meaning "shower". It follows the possessive adjective "sa" to complete the phrase.</div>','<h2>Il se lave les mains avant de manger.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>se</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun that is part of a reflexive verb "se laver". It refers back to the subject of the sentence, meaning that the subject performs the action on itself.</div><div><strong>lave</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "laver", which means "to wash". When combined with "se", the meaning changes to "washes himself/herself".</div><div><strong>les mains</strong> This phrase means "the hands" in English. "Les" is the definite article for plural nouns, and "mains" is the plural form of "main", which means "hand".</div><div><strong>avant</strong> This is a preposition that means "before" in English. It indicates that the action of washing hands occurs prior to another action.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is another preposition which, in this context, is used in conjunction with "avant" to form the expression "avant de", meaning "before" doing an infinitive verb/action.</div><div><strong>manger</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "manger", which means "to eat" in English.</div>','<h2>Je suis devant toi.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>suis</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes the state or action of the subject. In this sentence, it is used to express location relative to the person addressed.</div><div><strong>devant</strong> This is a French preposition that means "in front of". It indicates the relative position of the speaker to the person being addressed.</div><div><strong>toi</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when referring to one person informally. It’s the object of the preposition "devant", indicating who the speaker is in front of.</div>','<h2>Nous sommes devant la maison.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong>    This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>sommes</strong>    This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes the state of being of the subject.</div><div><strong>devant</strong>    This is a French preposition that means "in front of". It indicates the location relative to the noun that follows.</div><div><strong>la</strong>    This is the definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It’s used here to specify the house being talked about and is feminine singular to agree with "maison".</div><div><strong>maison</strong>    This is a French noun meaning "house" or "home". It is feminine singular and is the object of the preposition "devant" in this sentence.</div>','<h2>Le parking est devant le magasin.</h2><div><strong>Le</strong> This is the French definite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It’s used here to specify a particular parking area.</div><div><strong>parking</strong> This is a French noun borrowed from English, meaning "parking lot" or "car park". It denotes a designated area where vehicles can be parked.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It links the subject "le parking" to its location in this sentence.</div><div><strong>devant</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "in front of" or "before". It indicates the location of the parking in relation to the store.</div><div><strong>le</strong> Again, this is the French definite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes "magasin" to indicate a specific store.</div><div><strong>magasin</strong> This is a French noun meaning "store" or "shop". It’s the place in relation to which the parking lot is located.</div>','<h2>Je suis devant l’école.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>suis</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In this sentence, it indicates the location or position of the subject in relation to another place.</div><div><strong>devant</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "in front of". It indicates the relative position of the speaker to the place mentioned.</div><div><strong>l’école</strong> This is a French noun meaning "the school". The article "l’" is a contraction of "le" or "la" (both meaning "the" in English) before a vowel or mute h, and it is used here to denote a specific school that the speaker is in front of.</div>','<h2>Tu es parti après moi.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the French personal pronoun for the second-person singular, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking informally to one person. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>es</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In this sentence, it’s combined with the past participle to form the compound past tense.</div><div><strong>parti</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "partir", which means "to leave". Because "tu" is a masculine singular pronoun, "parti" is in the masculine singular form. If the subject was female, it would be "partie".</div><div><strong>après</strong> This is a preposition in French meaning "after". It indicates a sequence of events or a position in time relative to another event.</div><div><strong>moi</strong> This is a strong (stressed) pronoun in French, equivalent to "me" in English. It is used after prepositions, here indicating the person who was left behind.</div>','<h2>Il se couche après avoir lu un peu.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>se</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun that, in this context, is part of the reflexive verb "se coucher", which means "to go to bed". It indicates that the subject performs the action to himself.</div><div><strong>couche</strong> This word is the third-person singular present indicative tense of the verb "coucher", which means "to lay down" or "to go to bed". As part of the verb phrase "se coucher", it means "goes to bed".</div><div><strong>après</strong> This is a preposition meaning "after" in English. It’s used to indicate that one action follows another.</div><div><strong>avoir</strong> This is an auxiliary verb meaning "to have" in English. Here, it is followed by "lu" to form the perfect infinitive "après avoir lu", which means "after having read".</div><div><strong>lu</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "lire", which means "to read". It’s used with the auxiliary verb "avoir" to form the compound tense, indicating a completed action.</div><div><strong>un peu</strong> This is a phrase meaning "a little" in English. It modifies "lu", indicating that the reading was done for a short duration.</div>','<h2>Il se repose après une longue journée de travail.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>se repose</strong> This is a reflexive verb form in the present tense of "reposer", which means "to rest". With "se" the verb reflects back on the subject, translating to "rests himself" or simply "rests".</div><div><strong>après</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "after". It indicates that the action of resting follows another event or period of time.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the indefinite article in French for feminine singular nouns, meaning "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>longue</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "long". It is in the feminine singular form to agree with the feminine singular noun "journée".</div><div><strong>journée</strong> This is a feminine singular noun in French that means "day". It is often used to refer to the duration of a day’s events, comparable to "day’s work" or "daytime" in English.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a French preposition used here to introduce the phrase "travail" and translates as "of" in English, implying possession or association.</div><div><strong>travail</strong> This is a masculine singular noun that means "work" or "labor" in English.</div>','<h2>Nous prenons l’autobus après l’école.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the French word for "we". It is the first-person plural pronoun and is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>prenons</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "prendre", which means "to take". In this sentence, it denotes the action that the subject, "nous", is performing.</div><div><strong>l’autobus</strong> This is a French masculine noun meaning "the bus". The article "l’" is a contraction of "le" due to the following word starting with a vowel. This is the direct object of the verb "prendre" in the sentence, indicating what we are taking.</div><div><strong>après</strong> This is the French preposition meaning "after". It’s used to express the time when the action takes place.</div><div><strong>l’école</strong> This is a French feminine noun meaning "school". The article "l’" is a contraction of "la" due to the following word starting with a vowel. This noun follows the preposition "après" to specify the time when the action of taking the bus happens.</div>','<h2>Tu es derrière le camion.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking informally to one person. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>es</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes the state of being or position of the subject.</div><div><strong>derrière</strong> This is a French preposition that means "behind". It indicates the position or location in relation to something else.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article for masculine singular nouns in French, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>camion</strong> This is a French noun meaning "truck". It is a masculine singular noun that refers to a large motor vehicle designed to transport cargo.</div>','<h2>Elle se cache derrière le parapluie.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>se cache</strong> This is a reflexive verb form meaning "hides herself". "Se" indicates that the action of the verb is performed by the subject on herself, and "cache" is the third-person singular present tense of the verb "cacher", which means "to hide".</div><div><strong>derrière</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "behind". It indicates the position of the subject in relation to another object.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article in French, masculine singular form, and translates to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>parapluie</strong> This is a noun in French meaning "umbrella". It is the object behind which the subject is hiding.</div>','<h2>Il a une fleur derrière son dos.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It serves as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>a</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". It signifies possession or existence of something related to the subject.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the indefinite feminine singular article in French, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English. It introduces the noun "fleur".</div><div><strong>fleur</strong> This is a feminine noun in French, meaning "flower" in English. It’s the object of the verb and what "Il" has.</div><div><strong>derrière</strong> This is a preposition in French which translates to "behind" in English. It describes the position of the flower in relation to "son dos".</div><div><strong>son</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French that means "his" or "her" in English. It denotes ownership of "dos" by the subject "Il".</div><div><strong>dos</strong> This is a masculine noun in French, meaning "back" as in the body part, in English. It’s the object of the preposition "derrière", completing the description of the flower’s location.</div>','<h2>Il fait du ski nautique derrière le bateau.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>fait</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "faire", which means "to do" or "to make". In this context, it is part of the expression "faire du ski nautique" meaning "to water ski".</div><div><strong>du ski nautique</strong> This phrase means "water skiing". "Du" is a partitive article used in French to express an unspecified amount of something, in this case, it is combined with "ski nautique", which is a masculine noun phrase for the activity of water skiing.</div><div><strong>derrière</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "behind". It indicates the position of one thing relative to another.</div><div><strong>le bateau</strong> This phrase means "the boat". "Le" is the definite article in French used with masculine singular nouns, and "bateau" is a masculine singular noun meaning "boat".</div>'];var defBonus = ['<h2>Je suis parti avant eux.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>suis</strong> This is the first-person singular conjugation of the verb "être" in the past compound tense (passé composé), which means "to be". In this tense, it acts as an auxiliary verb to help form the past tense.</div><div><strong>parti</strong> This is the past participle of the French verb "partir", which means "to leave". When used with the auxiliary verb "être" in the passé composé, it denotes a completed action and agrees in gender and number with the subject. It is in the masculine singular form here to agree with "je".</div><div><strong>avant</strong> This is the French preposition meaning "before". It indicates a temporal relation between two actions, with this action happening before the other.</div><div><strong>eux</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "them" in English. It indicates the people who are being left or the ones that the subject left before.</div>','<h2>Avant onze heures, si possible.</h2><div><strong>Avant</strong> This is the French preposition for "before". It indicates the timing of an event in relation to another reference point in time.</div><div><strong>onze</strong> This is the number "eleven" in French. In this context, it signifies the hour of the day — 11 o’clock.</div><div><strong>heures</strong> This is the French word for "hours". In the context of time, it is often used to indicate the specific hour on the clock.</div><div><strong>si</strong> This is the French word for "if". It introduces a conditional statement or a possibility.</div><div><strong>possible</strong> This is an adjective meaning "possible" in French. It describes something that can be done or achieved, and here it signifies the speaker’s preference for something to occur before a certain time if it can happen.</div>','<h2>Je me réveillais toujours avant lui.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>me</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun in French, used here as part of the reflexive verb "se réveiller", which means "to wake up (oneself)". It indicates that the subject is performing the action on itself.</div><div><strong>réveillais</strong> This is the first-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "réveiller", used in its reflexive form "se réveiller". It indicates a continuous or habitual past action, translated as "was waking up" or "used to wake up".</div><div><strong>toujours</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "always". It describes the frequency with which the action of the verb occurs.</div><div><strong>avant</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "before". It indicates temporal order in relation to another event or time.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is a strong pronoun in French that can mean "him" or "her" based on context. In this sentence, it means "him", referring to another person (male) other than the speaker. It is the object of the preposition "avant".</div>','<h2>Mais il reste encore un mois avant les congés.</h2><div><strong>Mais</strong> This is the French word for "but". It is used to introduce a contrasting element or exception to what has been previously stated.</div><div><strong>il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" or "it" in English. In this sentence, it serves as a dummy subject, a grammatical necessity with no direct translation in this context.</div><div><strong>reste</strong> This comes from the French verb "rester", which means "to remain". "Reste" is the third-person singular present tense form, meaning "remains" or "is left".</div><div><strong>encore</strong> This French adverb translates to "still" or "yet" in English. It is used to indicate that something is continuing to happen up to a certain point or time.</div><div><strong>un mois</strong> This means "one month" in English. "Mois" is masculine and is preceded by an indefinite article "un", which means "one" for masculine nouns.</div><div><strong>avant</strong> A preposition in French which means "before". It indicates an event that will occur prior to another specified time or event.</div><div><strong>les congés</strong> This is a French noun meaning "the holidays" or "the vacation". It is in the plural form with the definite article "les", which means "the". "Congés" can refer to a period of time when one is exempt from work or other duties.</div>','<h2>Elle doit attacher ses lacets avant de courir.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>doit</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "devoir", which means "must" or "has to". It indicates an obligation or necessity.</div><div><strong>attacher</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb which means "to tie" or "to fasten". In this sentence, it’s used to describe the action that she needs to perform.</div><div><strong>ses</strong> This is a possessive adjective that means "her" in English (its form depends on the gender and number of the object possessed, not the subject). It’s used here to denote ownership of the "lacets".</div><div><strong>lacets</strong> This is a masculine plural noun which means "laces", referring to the laces in shoes.</div><div><strong>avant de</strong> This phrase translates to "before" in English, it is used to express an action that should take place prior to another action.</div><div><strong>courir</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb that means "to run" in English. It indicates the action that is intended to take place after tying the laces.</div>','<h2>Il n’avait jamais pris l’avion avant aujourd’hui.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>n’avait</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the auxiliary verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this sentence, it is used to express a past tense negative construction when combined with "ne...jamais" (never).</div><div><strong>jamais</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "never". It’s used after "ne" in negative sentences to negate the verb, indicating that an action has not occurred at any time.</div><div><strong>pris</strong> This is the past participle of the French verb "prendre", which means "to take". It’s used here with the auxiliary verb "avoir" to form the past compound tense known as the passé composé.</div><div><strong>l’avion</strong> This is a combination of the definite article "l’" (the) and the noun "avion", which means "airplane" in English. Together, they mean "the airplane".</div><div><strong>avant</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "before". It’s used to indicate a time reference.</div><div><strong>aujourd’hui</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "today". It’s combined with "avant" to indicate that the action has never occurred before this specific day.</div>','<h2>Ils buvaient du jus d’orange avant de se lever.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence and refers to a group of people.</div><div><strong>buvaient</strong> This is the third-person plural imperfect tense form of the verb "boire", which means "to drink". It indicates a past continuous or habitual action.</div><div><strong>du</strong> This is a partitive article in French, used here to express an unspecified quantity. It’s equivalent to "some" in English, as in "some juice".</div><div><strong>jus</strong> This is a French noun meaning "juice".</div><div><strong>d’orange</strong> This phrase is made up of the preposition "de" (contracted with the masculine singular definite article to "d’") and the noun "orange". It translates to "orange" in English, designating the type of juice they drunk.</div><div><strong>avant</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "before".</div><div><strong>de</strong> This preposition is part of the phrase "avant de", which is used before an infinitive to mean "before doing something".</div><div><strong>se lever</strong> This is the reflexive form of the verb "lever", which means "to get up". In this sentence, "se lever" translates to "to get up", and it’s in the infinitive form because it follows the preposition "de".</div>','<h2>Ils avaient voté avant le début des élections.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "They" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>avaient</strong> This is the third-person plural form of the imperfect tense of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". Here, it is used to describe an action that had been completed in the past, before another past action.</div><div><strong>voté</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "voter", which means "to vote". It is used here with the auxiliary verb "avaient" to form the plus-que-parfait (pluperfect) tense, which describes an action that had occurred before another past action.</div><div><strong>avant</strong> This is a preposition that means "before" in English. It is used to indicate that the action of voting took place before another event.</div><div><strong>le début</strong> This phrase combines the definite article "le" (meaning "the") and the noun "début" (meaning "start" or "beginning"). Together, they mean "the beginning".</div><div><strong>des élections</strong> This phrase contains the preposition "de" combined with the definite article "les" (contracting to "des") and the noun "élections" (meaning "elections"). Altogether, "des élections" means "of the elections" or "the elections" in the context of this sentence.</div>','<h2>Nous avons quelques minutes avant notre pause déjeuner.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>avons</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". Here, it indicates possession or in this context the amount of time we have.</div><div><strong>quelques</strong> This French word means "some" or "a few". It’s used here to indicate a small quantity or number of minutes.</div><div><strong>minutes</strong> This is the same as in English, meaning a unit of time equal to sixty seconds. It’s in the plural form because it’s preceded by "quelques", indicating more than one minute.</div><div><strong>avant</strong> This is a French preposition that means "before". It indicates that something is going to happen at a time earlier than the event discussed.</div><div><strong>notre</strong> This possessive adjective means "our" in English. It’s used to show that the lunch break belongs to or is associated with "nous" (we).</div><div><strong>pause</strong> This is equivalent to the English word "break". In French, it can refer to a short period of rest or interruption of activity.</div><div><strong>déjeuner</strong> This French word means "lunch". It’s a compound word that originally meant "to break the fast", coming from the verb "déjeuner", which means "to have breakfast" but in modern French mainly means "to have lunch".</div>','<h2>Il attachait ses lacets avant le match de tennis.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>attachait</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "attacher", which means "to tie". It describes an action that was ongoing or habitual in the past.</div><div><strong>ses</strong> This is a possessive adjective that translates to "his" or "her" in English. It indicates that the laces belong to the subject of the sentence (in this case "Il" — "he").</div><div><strong>lacets</strong> This is the plural form of the French word "lacet", which means "laces". Here, it refers to shoelaces.</div><div><strong>avant</strong> This is a preposition that translates to "before" in English. It’s used to indicate a time frame in relation to another event.</div><div><strong>le match</strong> This translates to "the match" in English, with "le" being the masculine singular definite article, and "match" being a masculine noun for a sports contest or game.</div><div><strong>de tennis</strong> The phrase translates as "of tennis", meaning the match mentioned is related to the sport of tennis. "De" is a preposition expressing possession or relation, and "tennis" is a masculine noun referring to the sport.</div>','<h2>Nous avions peint la chambre de notre nouveau bébé avant l’hiver.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>avions peint</strong> This is the pluperfect tense of the verb "peindre", which means "to paint". The construction of this tense (avoir in the imperfect + past participle of the main verb) conveys an action that was completed in the past, relative to another event in the past.</div><div><strong>la chambre</strong> This phrase means "the bedroom". "La" is a definite article in French, representing "the" in English, and "chambre" is a feminine noun meaning "room" or "bedroom".</div><div><strong>de notre nouveau bébé</strong> This phrase means "of our new baby". "De" is a preposition meaning "of", "notre" is a possessive adjective meaning "our", and "nouveau bébé" is a compound noun phrase meaning "new baby".</div><div><strong>avant</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "before". It’s used to indicate a time relation between two events.</div><div><strong>l’hiver</strong> This means "the winter". "L’" is the definite article used before vowels or mute "h", and "hiver" is a masculine noun meaning "winter".</div>','<h2>Il faudra qu’il attende un peu avant d’avoir un vrai camion.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It is the subject of the sentence here.</div><div><strong>faudra</strong> This is the third-person singular future tense of the verb "falloir", which means "to have to" or "must". In this case, it indicates necessity or requirement for the future action.</div><div><strong>qu’il</strong> This is a contraction of "que" (that) and "il" (he). Combined, they introduce a subordinate clause and act as the subject within that clause.</div><div><strong>attende</strong> This is the third-person singular present subjunctive form of the verb "attendre", which means "to wait". The subjunctive mood is used because the action is viewed as subjective or uncertain.</div><div><strong>un peu</strong> This phrase means "a little" in English, indicating a short amount of time.</div><div><strong>avant</strong> This means "before" in English, used as a preposition to indicate what must precede something else.</div><div><strong>d’avoir</strong> This is a combination of "de" (of, from) and "avoir" (to have), creating the infinitive phrase "d’avoir", which means "to have" in English, indicating the purpose or result to be achieved after waiting.</div><div><strong>un vrai</strong> "Un" is the masculine singular indefinite article "a" in English, and "vrai" is a French adjective that means "real" or "true". Together, they describe the object that follows, implying authenticity or genuineness.</div><div><strong>camion</strong> This is a French noun meaning "truck". In this sentence, it is the direct object of what he will eventually obtain.</div>','<h2>Il faudra que je soie patiente avant que mon arbre soit aussi grand.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the French impersonal pronoun, equivalent to the English "it" when used as in "it is necessary" or "one must". Here, it is used as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>faudra</strong> This is the future tense of the verb "falloir", which means "to have to" or "must". In this instance, it expresses necessity or requirement for what follows in the sentence.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This conjunction is used here to introduce the subordinate clause "I must be patient" ("je soie patiente").</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>soie</strong> This is the first-person singular present subjunctive form of the verb "être", which means "to be". The subjunctive mood is used after "que" since the statement expresses necessity.</div><div><strong>patiente</strong> This is an adjective meaning "patient". It is in feminine form to agree with the speaker—if she is female. If the speaker were male, it would be "patient".</div><div><strong>avant que</strong> This means "before" in English. It’s a conjunction used to introduce a time clause.</div><div><strong>mon</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "my". It indicates ownership or relation to the speaker and precedes a masculine noun.</div><div><strong>arbre</strong> This is a French noun meaning "tree".</div><div><strong>soit</strong> This is the third-person singular present subjunctive form of the verb "être" ("to be"). Again, the subjunctive mood is used after the conjunction "avant que".</div><div><strong>aussi</strong> This is an adverb meaning "also" or "as" in English. It’s being used here to indicate similarity in size.</div><div><strong>grand</strong> This is an adjective meaning "big" or "tall". It’s in masculine form to agree with "arbre", which is masculine in French. If the noun were feminine, it would be "grande".</div>','<h2>Tu prends ton café devant ton ordinateur.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It is used when speaking informally to one person and acts as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>prends</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "prendre", which means "to take" or "to have" in the context of food or drinks. Here, it suggests the action of having or consuming.</div><div><strong>ton café</strong> "<strong>ton</strong>" is the possessive adjective for second-person singular, meaning "your". "<strong>café</strong>" means "coffee", and together they mean "your coffee".</div><div><strong>devant</strong> This is a French preposition that means "in front of". It denotes location or position relative to something else.</div><div><strong>ton ordinateur</strong> "<strong>ton</strong>" is again the possessive adjective for second-person singular, meaning "your". "<strong>ordinateur</strong>" means "computer", and together they refer to "your computer".</div>','<h2>Nous verrons quand nous enlèverons nos mains devant nos yeux.</h2><div><strong>Nous verrons</strong> This phrase combines the subject pronoun "nous", meaning "we", with the future tense of the verb "voir", meaning "to see". It translates to "we will see", indicating an action that will take place in the future.</div><div><strong>quand</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "when". It introduces a subordinate clause and sets the timing for the action described in the main clause.</div><div><strong>nous</strong> Again, this is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It is the subject of the subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>enlèverons</strong> This is the first-person plural future tense form of the verb "enlever", which means "to remove" or "to take off". It denotes the action that the subject will perform in the future.</div><div><strong>nos</strong> This is the possessive adjective "our", which indicates possession and modifies "mains", meaning "hands".</div><div><strong>mains</strong> This is a French noun meaning "hands". It is in the plural form to match the plural possessive adjective "nos".</div><div><strong>devant</strong> This is a French preposition that translates to "in front of". It indicates the location in relation to "nos yeux".</div><div><strong>nos yeux</strong> This phrase combines the possessive adjective "nos", meaning "our", with the plural noun "yeux", meaning "eyes". Together, they mean "our eyes".</div>','<h2>La plage est après le pont?</h2><div><strong>La</strong> This is the French definite article for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes the noun "plage" in this sentence.</div><div><strong>plage</strong> This is a French noun meaning "beach". It is feminine and singular to match the definite article "la".</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be" in English. It links the subject "la plage" to the information that comes after it.</div><div><strong>après</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "after". It indicates that the location of "la plage" is following something else in the context given, which is "le pont".</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the French definite article for masculine singular nouns, similar to "the" in English. It precedes the noun "pont" in this sentence.</div><div><strong>pont</strong> This is a French noun meaning "bridge". It is masculine and singular to match the definite article "le". The sentence is asking if the beach is located after the bridge.</div>','<h2>Ils se coucheront après l’histoire.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "They" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence referring to a group of people or masculine and feminine mixed group.</div><div><strong>se coucheront</strong> This is the future tense of the reflexive verb "se coucher", which means "to go to bed" or "to lie down". The reflexive pronoun "se" indicates that the action is being performed by the subjects upon themselves.</div><div><strong>après</strong> This is a preposition meaning "after" in English. It’s used to express that one action will take place following another action or event.</div><div><strong>l’histoire</strong> This is a contraction of "la" (the) and "histoire" (story/history). In this context, it means "story". It is a singular feminine noun and, along with the article, translates to "the story" in English.</div>','<h2>Nous parlerons après la promenade à vélo.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the future tense verb in the sentence.</div><div><strong>parlerons</strong> This is the first-person plural future tense form of the verb "parler", which means "to speak" or "to talk". Here it suggests that the action will take place in the future.</div><div><strong>après</strong> This is a preposition in French that means "after". It is used to indicate that one action or event follows another.</div><div><strong>la promenade</strong> This is the French noun for "the walk" or "the ride", derived from the verb "promener" meaning "to walk" or "to take out". In this context, it specifies the activity that will precede the speaking.</div><div><strong>à vélo</strong> This phrase means "by bike" or "on a bike". "à" is a preposition and "vélo" is a noun meaning "bike". This phrase specifies the means of the promenade.</div>','<h2>Elle se couchait après avoir lu un peu.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular female pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>se couchait</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the reflexive verb "se coucher", which means "to go to bed" or "to lie down". The reflexive pronoun "se" indicates that the subject is performing the action on herself.</div><div><strong>après</strong> This is a preposition in French meaning "after". It is used to indicate that one action follows another.</div><div><strong>avoir</strong> This is an auxiliary verb in French meaning "to have". In this sentence, it’s used in the infinitive form as part of the past infinitive construction "après avoir" + [past participle], which expresses what the subject did before another action.</div><div><strong>lu</strong> This is the past participle of the French verb "lire", which means "to read". The past infinitive "avoir lu" means "after having read".</div><div><strong>un peu</strong> This phrase means "a little" in English. It describes the extent of the reading.</div>','<h2>Tu sécheras tes vêtements après les avoir lavés.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when addressing one person informally.  Here, it acts as the subject of the sentence. </div><div><strong>sécheras</strong> This is the second-person singular future tense of the verb "sécher", which means "to dry". It indicates an action you will perform in the future.</div><div><strong>tes</strong> This is the possessive adjective in the second-person singular, which means "your". It is used to denote possession of the clothes by the person being addressed.</div><div><strong>vêtements</strong> This is a French noun meaning "clothes". It is in the plural form to match the plural possessive adjective "tes".</div><div><strong>après</strong> This is a conjunction/preposition that means "after" in English. It introduces the following clause that mentions the action performed after another action.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the definite article in the plural form, meaning "the". It refers to the clothes (vêtements) that have already been mentioned.</div><div><strong>avoir</strong> This verb means "to have" in English. In this context, it’s an auxiliary verb used to form the past infinitive "avoir lavés".</div><div><strong>lavés</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "laver", which means "to wash". It is used in agreement with the noun "vêtements" and combined with "avoir" to express the action of having washed the clothes.</div>','<h2>Les enfants leur couraient après, c’était hilarant.</h2><div><strong>Les</strong> This is the definite article in French, plural form, meaning "the". It precedes the noun "enfants" to indicate a specific group being talked about.</div><div><strong>enfants</strong> This is a French noun meaning "children". It is in the plural form to indicate more than one child. This is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>leur</strong> This is an indirect object pronoun in French. It means "to them" or "for them" and refers to whom the children are running after.</div><div><strong>couraient</strong> This is the third-person plural imperfect tense form of the verb "courir", which means "to run". This tense is used to describe an ongoing past action.</div><div><strong>après</strong> This is a preposition in French meaning "after". It’s used in this phrase to indicate the direction of the children’s running.</div><div><strong>c’était</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense of the verb "être", meaning "it was". "C’était" serves as a linking verb to the adjective "hilarant".</div><div><strong>hilarant</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "hilarious". It describes the situation being recounted. The adjective agrees in gender and number with what it modifies, which in French is not placed so the agreement is in the masculine singular by default.</div>','<h2>Tu sauras nager après ton cours de natation.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking informally to one person. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>sauras</strong> This is the second-person singular future tense form of the verb "savoir", which means "to know". In this context, it means "will know" or "will be able to".</div><div><strong>nager</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "nager", which means "to swim". It is used here to indicate the action that "tu" (you) will be able to do.</div><div><strong>après</strong> This is a preposition meaning "after" in English. It indicates that the ability to swim comes after a certain time or event.</div><div><strong>ton</strong> This is the possessive adjective "your" in English. It is the masculine singular form to agree with the noun "cours".</div><div><strong>cours</strong> This is a French noun meaning "course" or "class" in English. When combined with "de natation", it refers specifically to a swimming class.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This preposition in French is often translated to "of" in English. Here, it’s used to link "cours" (course) with "natation" (swimming), describing the type of course.</div><div><strong>natation</strong> This is the French word for "swimming". It is a noun that refers to the activity being learned or practiced in the "cours" (course).</div>','<h2>Il est arrivé chez lui après une longue journée de travail.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence referring to a male individual.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In this context, it combines with the past participle "arrivé" to form the passé composé, a past tense expressing a completed action.</div><div><strong>arrivé</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "arriver", which means "to arrive". When combined with "est", it indicates that the subject has arrived at a certain point in time.</div><div><strong>chez</strong> This is a French preposition that means "at the home of". It indicates a location, specifically someone’s house.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is a disjunctive or stressed pronoun in French, equivalent to "him" in English. In this sentence, it’s used after "chez" to indicate "at his (own) place".</div><div><strong>après</strong> This is a preposition meaning "after" in English. It indicates that the action took place subsequent to another event.</div><div><strong>une longue</strong> This is a phrase consisting of an indefinite article "une", meaning "a", and the adjective "longue", which means "long". "Une longue" modifies the noun "journée" to describe the duration of the day.</div><div><strong>journée</strong> This is a feminine noun meaning "day". In this context, it refers to the duration of time spent working, similar to "workday" in English.</div><div><strong>de travail</strong> This phrase includes the preposition "de", meaning "of", and the noun "travail", which means "work". "De travail" functions as a complement to "journée" to specify the type of day, namely a "day of work" or "workday".</div>','<h2>Je déciderai après avoir fait un peu de recherche.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>déciderai</strong> This is the first-person singular future tense form of the verb "décider", which means "to decide". It indicates that the speaker will make a decision at some future point.</div><div><strong>après</strong> This is a preposition meaning "after" in English. It is used to indicate what happens following an event or action.</div><div><strong>avoir</strong> This is an auxiliary verb meaning "to have" in English. It is used here in the infinitive form as part of the perfect infinitive construction "avoir fait" which translates to "having done".</div><div><strong>fait</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "faire", which means "to do" or "to make" in English. In combination with "avoir", it forms the past infinitive "having done".</div><div><strong>un peu</strong> This phrase means "a little" in English. It indicates a small or moderate amount of something.</div><div><strong>de</strong> It is a preposition that can mean several things including "of" or "from" in English. In this context, it contributes to the phrase "un peu de recherche", meaning "a little bit of research".</div><div><strong>recherche</strong> This is a noun meaning "research" in English. It signifies the act of investigating or looking for information.</div>','<h2>Nous boirons une bouteille d’eau après nous être exercées.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>boirons</strong> This is the first-person plural future tense form of the verb "boire", which means "to drink". This indicates an action that will happen in the future.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the French indefinite article for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" in English.</div><div><strong>bouteille</strong> This is a French noun meaning "bottle". It is feminine and singular to match the article "une".</div><div><strong>d’eau</strong> This phrase means "of water" in English, where "de" is the preposition for "of", and "eau" is the noun for "water". In this context, it specifies what the bottle contains. "D’eau" is used instead of "de l’eau" because the preposition "de" contracts with the definite article "l’" before vowel sounds.</div><div><strong>après</strong> This is the French preposition meaning "after". It indicates what follows in time.</div><div><strong>nous être exercées</strong> This phrase means "having exercised ourselves" in English. "Nous" here acts as a reflexive pronoun, "être" is the infinitive of the verb "to be", and "exercées" is the past participle of "exercer" (to exercise) in its feminine plural form, indicating that the speaker is female and speaking on behalf of a group consisting of women.</div>','<h2>Elle ira regarder la télévision après avoir fait ses devoirs.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This word is the third-person singular feminine pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ira</strong> This is the future tense of the verb "aller", which means "to go". In this case, it’s used to express a future action she will do: "she will go".</div><div><strong>regarder</strong> This verb means "to watch". It is the infinitive form used here as part of a verb phrase indicating what action she will do in the future.</div><div><strong>la télévision</strong> This phrase means "the television". It is the direct object of the verb "regarder", indicating what she will watch.</div><div><strong>après</strong> This is a preposition that means "after". It introduces a temporal phrase indicating when the action of watching will take place.</div><div><strong>avoir</strong> This verb means "to have". In this context, it’s part of the past infinitive "avoir fait", which is a compound verb structure used to express an action that will be completed in the future before another action occurs.</div><div><strong>fait</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "faire", which means "to do" or "to make". It’s used here with "avoir" to form the past infinitive "having done".</div><div><strong>ses</strong> This is a possessive adjective that means "her". It matches the gender and number of the noun it modifies, which in this case is "devoirs".</div><div><strong>devoirs</strong> This is the plural noun which means "homework". It’s the direct object of the phrase "avoir fait", telling us what activity she will have completed before watching television.</div>'];definitions = definitions.concat(defBonus);